{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 1839,
    "dDurationMs": 4081,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you take a piece of wood and put it next\nto another piece of wood... nothing happens."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5920,
    "dDurationMs": 4220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And if you take a piece of granite and put\nit next to another rock... still nothing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10140,
    "dDurationMs": 4270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if you take this piece of iron and put\nit next to this other piece of iron... magic!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 14410,
    "dDurationMs": 1729,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I mean, magnet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 16139,
    "dDurationMs": 4341,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Magnetic objects are able to magically attract\nat long distance because they generate magnetic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20480,
    "dDurationMs": 2930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fields that extend invisibly out beyond the\nobject."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 23410,
    "dDurationMs": 3640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the mystery is this: where do magnetic\nfields come from?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 27050,
    "dDurationMs": 1620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Derek: well that's easy, Henry!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 28670,
    "dDurationMs": 3949,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We've known for a long time that electricity\nand magnetism are really just two sides of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 32619,
    "dDurationMs": 5141,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the same coin, kind of like mass and energy\nor time and space, and they can be transformed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 37760,
    "dDurationMs": 1030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into each other."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 38790,
    "dDurationMs": 3940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact, magnetic fields are basically just\nwhat electric fields turn into when an electrically"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 42730,
    "dDurationMs": 1660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "charged object starts moving!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44390,
    "dDurationMs": 3620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Henry: That makes sense for explaining why\na current of electrons flowing through a wire"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48010,
    "dDurationMs": 4050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "causes this compass needle to move, or how\ncurrents in the earth's outer core generate"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 52060,
    "dDurationMs": 4330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the geomagnetic field... but a bar magnet\nor the compass needle itself are just pieces"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 56390,
    "dDurationMs": 2620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of metal without any electrical current running\nthrough them."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 59010,
    "dDurationMs": 1029,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Derek: Or are they?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60039,
    "dDurationMs": 3791,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At a microscopic level, there are loads of\nelectrons whizzing around in the atoms and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 63830,
    "dDurationMs": 1730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "molecules that make up any solid."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65560,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Henry: Right!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66560,
    "dDurationMs": 4599,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This brings up an excellent point - The magnetic\nbehavior of any everyday object is influenced"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 71159,
    "dDurationMs": 4820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by a fascinating combination of effects ranging\nfrom the level of particles to atoms, collections"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 75979,
    "dDurationMs": 2941,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of atoms, and collections of collections of\natoms."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78920,
    "dDurationMs": 2049,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First, individual particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 80969,
    "dDurationMs": 4101,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Unlike the everyday workings of gravity and\nelectricity, permanent magnets can only be"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85070,
    "dDurationMs": 2939,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fully understood as a quantum mechanical effect."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 88009,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In much the same way that particles like electrons\nand quarks have fundamental properties called"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92149,
    "dDurationMs": 4780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass and electrical charge, most particles\nALSO have another intrinsic property, called"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96929,
    "dDurationMs": 1530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\"tiny magnet\"."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98459,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Just kidding, it's called an \"intrinsic magnetic\nmoment,\" but really, that's just technical"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 102219,
    "dDurationMs": 4150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mumbo-jumbo saying that particles with electric\ncharge ALSO happen to be tiny magnets."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 106369,
    "dDurationMs": 4471,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Derek: If you want to know WHY they're tiny\nmagnets, well, you might as well ask WHY do"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 110840,
    "dDurationMs": 3889,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particles have charge in the first place,\nor why do objects with energy and momentum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 114729,
    "dDurationMs": 2081,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "attract gravitationally?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 116810,
    "dDurationMs": 1349,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "No one knows..."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 118159,
    "dDurationMs": 2170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We just know these things are true/that is\nhow the universe works."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 120329,
    "dDurationMs": 4691,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Henry: Exactly, and since the 1920s, we've\nknown that each individual electron or proton"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 125020,
    "dDurationMs": 1739,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is basically a tiny magnet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 126759,
    "dDurationMs": 2261,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which brings us to the level of atoms."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 129020,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "An atom is a bunch of positively charged protons\nwith a bunch of negatively charged electrons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 133500,
    "dDurationMs": 1129,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "whizzing around them."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 134629,
    "dDurationMs": 4941,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The proton tiny magnets are about 1000 times\nweaker than the electron ones, so the nucleus"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 139570,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the atom has almost no effect on the magnetism\nof the atom as a whole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 143250,
    "dDurationMs": 4079,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Derek: And you might think that since many\n(though not all) of the electrons are also"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 147329,
    "dDurationMs": 5761,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "moving, like the current in a wire, they would\ngenerate magnetic fields from that motion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 153090,
    "dDurationMs": 3410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Indeed they do, and these are called \"orbital\"\nmagnetic fields."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 156500,
    "dDurationMs": 3790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Henry: Except, these don't usually contribute\nto the magnetic field of an atom."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 160290,
    "dDurationMs": 1380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Here's why:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 161670,
    "dDurationMs": 4580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Electrons in atoms are accurately and complicatedly\ndescribed by quantum mechanics, but the gist"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 166250,
    "dDurationMs": 3969,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the story is that electrons congregate\nin shells around the nucleus."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 170219,
    "dDurationMs": 4270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The electrons in any filled shell zoom equally\nin all directions and so the currents they"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 174489,
    "dDurationMs": 2881,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "generate cancel out and generate no magnetic\nfield."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 177370,
    "dDurationMs": 4580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These electrons also come in pairs whose tiny\nmagnets point in opposite directions and also"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 181950,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cancel."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 182950,
    "dDurationMs": 4420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However, in a half-filled shell, all of the\nelectrons are unpaired and their tiny magnets"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 187370,
    "dDurationMs": 4489,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "point in the same direction and add up, meaning\nthat it's the intrinsic magnetism of the electrons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 191859,
    "dDurationMs": 3671,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the outer shell that gives an atom the\nmajority of its magnetic field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 195530,
    "dDurationMs": 4780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So atoms near the side of any of the major\nblocks of the periodic table, which have full"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 200310,
    "dDurationMs": 3399,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "(or nearly full) outer electron shells, aren't\nvery magnetic."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 203709,
    "dDurationMs": 4991,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And atoms in the MIDDLE of the blocks have\nhalf-full outer electron shells and are magnetic."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 208700,
    "dDurationMs": 3569,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, Nickel, Cobalt, Iron, Manganese,\nChromium, etc."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 212269,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Derek: Wait, but chromium isn't magnetic!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 214909,
    "dDurationMs": 4660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Henry: Ah, but just because an atom is magnetic\ndoesn't mean that a material made up of lots"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 219569,
    "dDurationMs": 2051,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of that atom will be magnetic."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 221620,
    "dDurationMs": 2369,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which brings us to the level of crystals."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 223989,
    "dDurationMs": 4821,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When a bunch of magnetic atoms get together\nto make a solid, they generally have two options."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 228810,
    "dDurationMs": 3649,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One is for all of the atoms to align their\nmagnetic fields with each other, or they can"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 232459,
    "dDurationMs": 3700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "align the magnetic fields in an alternating\nfashion so that they all cancel out."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 236159,
    "dDurationMs": 2261,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The atoms will do whichever one requires less\nenergy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 238420,
    "dDurationMs": 4670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Derek: That's why chromium, for example, is\na very magnetic atom but a very un-magnetic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 243090,
    "dDurationMs": 4989,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "solid - because it's one of the most anti-ferromagnetic\nmaterials we know."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 248079,
    "dDurationMs": 5451,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Iron, on the other hand, is the name-sake\nof ferromagnetism, so it is, unsurprisingly,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 253530,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ferromagnetic."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 254530,
    "dDurationMs": 4390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or, in usual parlance: magnetic."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 258920,
    "dDurationMs": 3380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Henry: Sometimes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 262300,
    "dDurationMs": 4190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The last and final level of magnetism is that\nof domains."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 266490,
    "dDurationMs": 4730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Essentially, even in a magnetic material where\nthe magnetic fields of atoms line up together,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 271220,
    "dDurationMs": 3390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it's possible that one chunk of the material\nwill have all its atoms lined up pointing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 274610,
    "dDurationMs": 4059,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one way, and another chunk will have all its\natoms pointing another way, and so on."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 278669,
    "dDurationMs": 5250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Derek: If all of these \"Domains\" are of approximately\nsimilar size, none may be strong enough to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 283919,
    "dDurationMs": 5391,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "force the others to align with it, and so\na piece of iron, for example, might have no"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 289310,
    "dDurationMs": 4530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "magnetic field because of all of the warring\nmagnetic kingdoms within it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 293840,
    "dDurationMs": 4079,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Henry: However, if you apply a strong enough\nmagnetic field/force/pressure from outside"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 297919,
    "dDurationMs": 4381,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the material, you can help favor one domain/help\none domain expand its control over its neighbors,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 302300,
    "dDurationMs": 4530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and so on until all of the domains have been\nunified into one kingdom, all pointing in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 306830,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the same direction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 307830,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Derek : And now, finally, you can rule with\nan iron fist..."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 311790,
    "dDurationMs": 3839,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I mean, magnet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 315629,
    "dDurationMs": 2571,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Henry: Exactly!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 318200,
    "dDurationMs": 4700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What's remarkable is that magnetism is a fundamentally\nquantum property amplified to the size of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 322900,
    "dDurationMs": 4949,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "everyday objects: every permanent magnet is\na reminder that quantum mechanics underlies"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 327849,
    "dDurationMs": 5841,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our universe - in order for any object to\nbe magnetic, it has to have a unified kingdom"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 333690,
    "dDurationMs": 4699,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of magnetic domains, each made up of bajillions\nof magnetic atoms which also need to be aligned"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 338389,
    "dDurationMs": 3981,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with each other, each of which can only be\nmagnetic in the first place if it has an approximately"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 342370,
    "dDurationMs": 4329,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "half-filled outer shell of electrons so their\nintrinsic magnetic fields can align and not"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 346699,
    "dDurationMs": 1411,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cancel each other out."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 348110,
    "dDurationMs": 4149,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Not surprisingly, these criteria are pretty\ndifficult to fulfill, which is why there are"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 352259,
    "dDurationMs": 3831,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "only a limited number of suitable materials\nyou can use when you're building a magnet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 356090,
    "dDurationMs": 3919,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Derek: OR you could just run a current through\nany electrical conductor and generate a magnetic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 360009,
    "dDurationMs": 1011,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field that way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 361020,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Henry: But hey..."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 362020,
    "dDurationMs": 1639,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Why does that work in the first place?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 363659,
    "dDurationMs": 3901,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Click here to go to over to Veritasium and\nwe'll find out what special relativity and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 367560,
    "dDurationMs": 4009,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the speed of light have to do with electromagnets."
    } ]
  } ]
}
